course: dictionary
lesson: 5316
title: Keepsakes and memories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A keepsake is a small thing you keep to remember a person, a place or a time. The words around it give clues. «A keepsake can be very precious even when it is worth little money, so you should ask before touching it.» Precious here means valued for its memories, not its price, because the sentence says it can be precious even when it is cheap.
explain: Think about what a keepsake might look like. «A keepsake is something that belonged to someone who died, kept to feel close to them, such as a ring, a jumper or a recipe book.» Those examples are ordinary things, so a keepsake is ordinary in shape but special in meaning. Try your meaning in the sentence: "a thing you keep to remember someone" fits well.
explain: A keepsake can also be a gift. «Vets often make a clay paw print or keep a lock of fur so families have a keepsake.» Here the keepsake is a way for a family to remember a pet. When you meet a word you do not know, read the sentence again and ask what the word is doing for the reader.
fact: A keepsake is a noun: a keepsake, keepsakes.
fact: «Keepsake» means a small thing you keep to help you remember a person, place or time.
fact: A keepsake can also be a gift given so the person will remember the giver.
fact: Keepsakes can be a photo, a shell from a holiday, or a ring from a grandparent who has died.
fact: Useful phrases: «as a keepsake», «a treasured keepsake», «keepsake box».
exercise: «A keepsake can be very precious even when it is worth little money.» What does keepsake mean in this sentence? | A letter sent to a friend | *A small thing you keep to remember someone or a time | A bag for carrying shopping | A large sum of money kept in the bank
exercise: Mum says the ring from Grandma is a keepsake. What does she mean? | It is a new ring from the shop | *It is precious because of the memories, not its price | It is a ring she borrowed from a neighbour | It is very expensive and should be sold
exercise: «Vets often make a clay paw print or keep a lock of fur so families have a keepsake.» Find and copy one word that means a thing kept to remember someone. | families | print | *keepsake | paw
test: «A keepsake is something that belonged to someone who died, kept to feel close to them.» Which of these could be a keepsake? | *A jumper that belonged to a grandad who has died | A carton of milk | A new jumper bought from the shop this morning | A bus ticket from last week
test: What would most likely be kept in a «keepsake box»? | Cleaning cloths and bottles of soap | Tools for fixing a bike | Boxes of cereal for breakfast | *Small treasured things that remind you of people and times
exam: Why is a keepsake precious? | Because it is heavy and hard to lift | *Because it reminds you of people and times, not because of its price | Because it is always made of gold | Because it is new and shiny
